## a b s t r a c t
Protective fabrics containing Zr-Based Metal-Organic Frameworks (Zr-MOFs) show great potential in the detoxification of chemical warfare agents (CWAs). However, the current studies still face the challenges of complicated fabrication processes, limited MOF loading mass, and insufficient protection. Herein, we developed a lightweight, flexible and mechanical robust aerogel by in situ growth of UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ onto aramid nanofibers (ANFs) and assembly of UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ loaded ANFs (UiO-66- $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.\mathrm{\textJH}_{2}@\mathrm{ANF}s\aftergroup\egroup\right.$ into 3D hierarchically porous architecture. The UiO-66- ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels feature high MOF loading of $261\%$ , high surface area of $589.349~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , open and interconnected cellular structure, which provide efficient transfer channels and promote catalytic degradation of CWAs. As a result, the UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels demonstrate high 2-chloroethyl ethyl thioether (CEES) removal rate at $98.9\%$ and a short half-life of $8.15\ \mathrm{min}$ Moreover, the aerogels present good mechanical stability (recovery rate of $93.3\ \%$ after 100 cycles under $30\%$ strain), low thermal conductivity ( $\lambda$ of $25.66\mathrm{\mW\m^{-1}\ K^{-1}},$ , high flame resistance (LOI of $32\%$ ) and good wearing comfortableness, indicating promising potential in multifunctional protection against CWAs.
