## 1. Introduction
Chemical warfare agents (CWAs) are highly toxic compounds that threaten public safety, military personnel and the environment, when emitted accidentally or deliberately [1,2]. Currently, activated carbons are commonly employed to remove toxic chemicals by adsorption from contaminated air, but such protection suffers from the drawbacks of heaviness, non-selective adsorption, and risk of secondary emission [3,4]. Therefore, self-detoxifying protective materials are urgently desired for their great potential to provide efficient protection [5].
Recently, metal organic frameworks (MOFs), which are composed of organic ligands and inorganic metal centers, have shown significant advantages in the degradation of CWAs, a feature attributed to their unique structure and abundant active sites. Among these MOFs, Zr-MOFs, such as UiO-66 [6], $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ [7] and MOF-808 [8], have attracted much attention because they can efficiently catalytically degrade CWAs through the hydrolysis of numerous Lewis-acidic $Z\mathrm{r}-0\mathrm{H}-Z\mathrm{r}$ bonds [9–11]. Furthermore, $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOF-functionalized textiles have been proposed for selfdetoxification by using various methods of physical spraying [12], layer-by-layer assembly [13], co-electrospinning [14] or atomic layer deposition (ALD) [15] assisted by the growth of MOFs on fibers. However, these MOF textiles still face the challenges of complicated fabrication processes, limited MOF loading mass, and insufficient protections.
Herein, we reported a lightweight, flexible and mechanically robust aerogel combining $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and aramid nanofibers (ANFs) for self-detoxification. ANFs with large aspect ratio and high specific surface areas (SSAs) are derived from poly $\dot{\boldsymbol{p}}$ -phenylene terephthalamide) (PPTA) fibers, one of the strongest polymer fiber with the well-known trademark of Kevlar. By in situ growth of UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ onto ANFs and assembly of UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ loaded ANFs (UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F S}$ ) into 3D hierarchically porous architecture, the obtained UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels (UiO-66- ${\tt-N H}_{2}@{\tt A N F A S}.$ ) feature high UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ loading of $261\%$ , an extremely high surface area of $589\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and low bulk density of $4.7\ \mathrm{mg/cm^{3}}$ , thereby generating, to the best of our knowledge, the lightest self-detoxification textile with the highest MOFs loading mass. As a proof of concept, our UiO-6 ${\mathsf{6-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F A s}}$ exhibit superior degrading activity against mustard gas simulant of 2-chloroethyl ethyl thioether (CEES) with a high removal rate of $98.9\%$ and short half-life of $8.15\ \mathrm{min}$ . Furthermore, UiO-66-NH2@ANFAs have good mechanical stability (recovery rate of $93.3\%$ after 100 cycles under $30\%$ strain), superior thermal insulation with low thermal conductivity of $25.66\mathrm{\mW}\mathrm{m}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , and fire resistance, and these features are requisites for practical applications.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
$\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{zr}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ $(\mathsf{A R},\mathsf{\Omega}\geq\mathsf{\Omega}98\mathsf{\Omega}\%$ , 2-aminobenzene-1,4- dicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{D}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}}$ , $\mathsf{A R},\mathsf{\Omega}\geq98\mathrm{\Omega}\%$ ), sodium hydroxide (NaOH, AR, $\geq98\ \%$ and 2-chloroethyl ethyl thioether (CEES, AR, $\geq97\mathrm{~\%}$ were purchased from Aladdin Reagent (China) Co., Ltd. N, $N-$ dimethylformamide (DMF, AR, $\geq99.5\mathrm{~\\%~}$ , methanol $\left(\mathsf{A R},\mathsf{\Omega}\geq\right.$ $99.5~\%$ ), acetonitrile $(\mathsf{C R},\geq99.9\%$ ), acetic acid $(\mathsf{A R},\geq99.5\ \%$ , and tert butyl alcohol (TBA, $A R,\geq99\mathrm{~\%}$ ) were purchased from Tianjin Kailis Fine Chemical Co., Ltd. Distilled water (electrical resistivity $<18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega\dot{}$ ) was purchased from Heal-force water meter. All chemicals were used as received without further purification. Blocked isocyanate (B-MDI) was synthesized as described in our previous work [16].
## 2.2. Fabrication of aramid nanofibers
PPTA staple fiber $(2{\bf\delta g})$ was added to $500~\mathrm{mL}$ of $15\ \%\ \mathsf{N a O H}$ solution, refluxed for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $80^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and washed thoroughly with distilled water. The pre-treated PPTA was dispersed in DI water and passed through a high-pressure homogenizer (UH-24, Union-biotech, China) for 30 cycles at $1000\mathrm{MPa}$ to obtain ANF dispersion.
## 2.3. Fabrication of aramid nanofibers aerogels
The ANF dispersion was prepared using an ice-template technology. The obtained ANF dispersion was regulated as $15\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ in a mixture of water and TBA (10:1 in vol), and $0.1\ \%$ B-MDI (accounting for fibers) was subsequently mixed. The dispersion was frozen at $-55^{\circ}C$ and freeze-dried for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ using a vacuum freeze-dryer (30YG/A, Ningbo Xinzhi Co., Ltd., China). Finally, the freshly freeze-dried aerogels were heated at $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ under a vacuum to obtain the flexible ANF aerogel.
## 2.4. Fabrication of UiO-66- $N H_{2}@A N F$ aerogels
2-amino-terephthalic acid ATA $(0.0496\mathrm{g})$ was first used to treat $_{0.064\mathrm{~g~}}$ of ANF in DMF, then $_{0.064\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ , $0.0496\mathrm{~g~}$ of ATA, $3.2~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid and $_{0.064\mathrm{~g~}}$ of the treated ANF was added into $16~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for in situ growth of UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@\mathrm{ANF}\right.$ at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ The UiO-6 ${\mathsf{i}}6{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels were fabricated with varied concentrations as described in Section 3.3.
## 2.5. Characterizations
SEM (Gemini SEM500) and TEM (Hitachi H7650) were used to examine the morphology. XPS (K-Alpha), FTIR (Nicolet iS50) and XRD (D8 ADVANCE) were employed to determine the chemical composition. TGA (STA 449F5) measurements were carried out at temperatures ranging from room temperature to $700^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . The automatic surface area and porosity analyzer (ASAP2460) was used to analyze BET and pore structures. The decontamination was monitored by GC–MS (Agilent Technologies 7890A). The heat insulation was performed by an infrared thermal imager (I7) and thermal conductivity meter (TPS2500S). The compression experiment was tested with an electronic universal testing machine (CMT6103).
