## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fabrication of UiO-66-NH2@ANF aerogels
To obtain the self-detoxification of CWAs, the ideal Zr-MOFs textiles should have the following characteristics: (i) the loading content of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOFs could be regulated, with potential to obtain enough Zr-MOF crystals mass loading for high catalysis；(ii) the textile could provide efficient transfer channels and promote catalytic degradation of CWAs; and (iii) the textile could achieve multifunctional protection for practical applications. To meet the prerequisites of a self-detoxification textile, the UiO-66- ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels were designed by adopting ANF aerogels for supporting $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ crystals. The significant difference between the UiO- ${\mathsf{i}}6{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel and the reported MOFsloaded textiles is that the aerogel demonstrates a 3D nanofibrous network with small diameter and an open and interconnected cellular structure. The nanoscale diameter would offer high specific surface area for loading MOF crystals, and the meso-/macropores of the ANF aerogel could act as gas transfer channels to facilitate the transport of CWAs for full exposure to the loaded MOF crystals.
For the fabrication of the ANF aerogels, PPTA fibers (Fig. S1) were first exfoliated into ANFs with size ranging from 50 to $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 1a) using high-pressure homogenization (HPH) process, as we reported previously [16].Given the good dispersion of ANFs in an aqueous solution, a $0.25\mathrm{\wt\%}$ ANF suspension with 0.1 wt% B-MDI as binding agent (accounting for ANFs) was freeze–dried at $-55^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Upon freezing, the ANFs and binding agent are repelled from the moving solidification front and assembly between the ice crystals. After the elimination of ice crystals during the following freeze–drying process and a curing treatment at 200 $^{\circ}C$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ , the ANF aerogel is obtained as a hierarchical nanofibrous network (Fig. 1b). The high porosity endows the obtained ANF aerogel with a low density of $2.7~\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , thereby allowing the $15~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ aerogel to stand on the tip of a green bristlegrass (Fig. 1c).
To obtain the UiO-6 ${6{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}}$ aerogel, ATA was added into the ANF suspension in DMF and homogenized for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ to obtain
ATA-absorbed ANFs (Fig. S2, Table S1), which are regarded as the nucleation points for the growth of UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ . As expected, the UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ is anchored onto ANFs with high loading mass (Fig. 1d) by the solvothermal method at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The typical UiO$66{\-{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F}}$ clearly demonstrates a uniform UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ layer covering ANF (Fig. 1e), a feature which is further confirmed by the uniform distribution of elements (Fig. S3). Remarkably, the obtained UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F s}}$ still exhibit good water dispersibility; thus, the UiO- ${\mathsf{S6-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel was fabricated using the similar procedure employed for the ANF aerogel. Despite the high loading of UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , UiO- ${\cdot66{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F s}}$ present a lightweight feature of a low density of $4.7~\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (Fig. 1f).
The loaded UiO-66- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ crystals can be easily regulated by adjusting the amount of the MOF precursor used. Fig. 2a-2d present the morphology of the aerogels with different contents of the MOF precursor (with $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{Cl}_{4}}$ content as 0.3, 0.5, 1 and 2 times that of the ANFs). The corresponding aerogels are designated as UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@\mathrm{ANFA-}x,\right.$ , where $x$ is the ratio of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ to ANFs. As shown in Fig. 2e, the loaded UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ crystals clearly increased with the increase of the MOF precursor, and the highest UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ content is up to $261\%$ for UiO-6 $\mathsf{\bar{s}}\mathsf{-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F A-}2$ .
The immobilization of UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ onto ANFs was confirmed by X-ray diffraction. As shown in Fig. 2f, typical diffraction peaks occurred at $7.27^{\circ}$ and $8.4^{\circ}$ of the UiO-6 ${6{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F A s}}}$ , and this outcome agrees well with those of the conventionally synthesized pure $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ powder. In the FTIR spectrum (Fig. S4), the UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F A s}}$ demonstrate COOH stretching peaks (3330, 1560, and $1200\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and $Z\mathrm{{r}}-0$ stretching vibration peaks $(764~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ [17,18].
The pore-texture of the UiO-66- $\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}@$ ANFAs was demonstrated by an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption measurement, as presented in Fig. 2g. Type I isotherms with steep $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ uptake in the low-pressure range $(\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}<0.1)$ were obtained for all of the UiO-6 ${\mathsf{G-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels. Type H4 hysteresis loops were observed for the aerogels in the high-pressure areas $\mathrm{(P/P_{0}}$ greater than 0.9), thereby indicating the introduction of massive mesopores/macropores from the assembly of ANFs. Remarkably, surface area as high as 381.319, 545.965 and 589.349 $\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~\bf~g^{-1}~}$ were obtained for UiO-66- ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathrm{ANFA}}-0.5$ , UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{2}@{\tt A N F A-1}$ and UiO-66- $\cdot\N{\mathrm{H}}_{2}@.$ ANFA-2, respectively (Table S2). The pore volume distributions suggest that all loaded UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ crystals are mainly microporous and the average pore size distribution was approximately $1.4\mathrm{-}1.5~\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2h and Table S2), indicating that the UiO- ${\mathopen{.66-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F A s}}$ contain an abundant microporous structure, similar to the previously reported UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ powder [19]. Thus, UiO- ${\mathsf{S6-N H}}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F}$ aerogels with hierarchically porous architecture and high MOF crystals loaded were fabricated.
## 3.2. Catalysis degradation of CEES
To evaluate the functional performance of the UiO-66- ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels, we performed catalytic degradation using CEES as the simulant of mustard gas, with gas chromatography (GC) detecting the process (Fig. S7), and the removal percentages of CEES of the UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F}\right.$ aerogels are presented in Fig. 3a. For UiO-66- $\cdot\N{\sf H}_{2}@$ ANFA-2, the removal rates reach more than 60 $\%$ within $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and more than $90\%$ at $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . Finally, the removal efficiencies after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ are $97.1\ \%$ , $98.7\ \%$ and $98.9\ \%$ for UiO-66- ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathrm{ANFA}}-0.5$ , UiO-66 $\J_{\mathsf{-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F A-1}}$ , and UiO- ${\tt S}6{\tt-N}{\tt H}_{2}@{\tt A N F A-2}$ , respectively. In Fig. 3b, the half-life values for CEES degradation using the aerogels are shown as 21.65, 10.5 and $8.15\ \mathrm{min}$ , respectively. As compared in Table 1, our UiO-66- ${\mathsf{\cdot N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels demonstrate higher removal efficiency and faster degradation rate with less UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ mass. The results confirm the feasibility of our design concept.
The degradation products were further examined by gas chromatography mass spectrometer (GC–MS). As shown in Fig. 3c and Fig.S8, three substances of CEES, ethyl-2-hydroxyethyl sulfide (HEES) and 1,2-Bis (ethylthio) ethane (BETE) were detected. Therefore, the inference can be made that CEES reduces its own toxicity primarily through both hydrolysis and fragment recombination [20]. Thus, the possible reactions are proposed as shown in Fig. 3d. The C-Cl bond in CEES breaks first to produce an intermediate, which may bind to $-{\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ to form HEES (Fig. 3d1), and the intermediate may also continue to undergo $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ fracture, and then undergo fragment recombination to form BETE (Fig. 3d2).
For practical application of the UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel, the material should be easy to reuse. As shown in Fig. 3e, the UiO
$66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F}\mathrm{-}1$ aerogel still retained more than $94\%$ of the degradation efficiency after three cycles. XRD and FT-IR analysis reveal that the reused UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{2}@{\tt A N F}{\bf-}1$ shows similar structure to the pristine aerogel (Fig. 3f, g), as confirmed by a SEM image (Fig. 3h).
## 3.3. Physical protection of the UiO-66-NH2@ANF aerogels
For practical military application, protective clothing probably faces the risk of fire; therefore, flame retardant and thermal insulation features are essential requirements. To evaluate the thermal insulation property, the UiO-6 ${6{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F A}}}$ of $12\ \mathrm{mm}$ thickness with different densities were prepared by regulating the concentration of UiO-6 ${6{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}}$ in dispersion. As shown in Fig. 4a, the aerogels fabricated with the concentration of $0.15\mathrm{~\%~}$ , $0.2\ \%$ and $0.25\ \%$ all demonstrated excellent thermal insulation. When kept on a hot stage of $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ , their surface temperatures were only $81.2^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ $92.5~^{\circ}C$ and $97.4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ respectively. The large temperature differences $(\Delta\mathsf{T})$ were up to $118.8\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ , $107.5~^{\circ}C$ and 102.6 $^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ respectively, as clearly demonstrated from the temperature curves (Fig. 4b). The thermal conductivities $(\lambda)$ of the aerogels were further measured as $25.66{-}28.14\mathrm{mW/mK}$ (Fig. 4c), which is closed to that of air and competitive relative to the reported thermal insulation materials. The thermal insulation property of the aerogel is determined by many factors [28–30]. Thermal convection and solid/air thermal conduction are the main ones of these factors. The thermal convection of the aerogel is greatly restricted as air is blocked within the micropores, mesopores, and macropores, Furthermore, the aerogel has high porosity, as air has a much lower thermal conduction than solid [31]. The limit oxygen index (LOI) was also tested as $32\%$ for the UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\right.$ ANFA-1, indicating excellent flame retardancy.
The mechanical property of the aerogel was then evaluated to test its tolerance to compression. As shown in Fig. 4d, the stress– stain $\scriptstyle({\upsigma}-{\upvarepsilon})$ curves in the loading process exhibit the three characteristic deformation stages typically found in cellular networks: when $\varepsilon<5\%$ , it is linear or in the Hookean elastic region. The compressive stress $\upsigma$ is almost constant, and the deformation is mainly caused by the flexibility of the fiber. A plateau stage of $5\%<$ $\varepsilon<40\ \%$ applies, in which $\upsigma$ increases linearly with the increase of e, indicating that its internal lumen structure is well maintained. Until e greater than $40\%$ , $\upsigma$ increases sharply, indicating that the internal interfiber pores are highly compressed and have become dense, but the structural integrity can still be maintained, thereby revealing that the interfiber is firmly bonded by physical/chemical interactions. This phenomenon is consistent with the typical compressive stress–strain curve of the elastic aerogel [32]. To assess the compression fatigue resistance of aerogel, we investigated the cycliccompression performance of the aerogel at $30\%$ strain.
The $\scriptstyle{\overline{{\sigma}}}-\varepsilon$ curve of the aerogel compressed by 100 times (Fig. 4e), and the UiO-66 $\mathsf{j}{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{A N F}$ aerogel presented a minor irreversible deformation (Fig. S5). The results illustrate the UiO- ${\mathsf{66-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel structural stability and durability, suggesting its practical and promising prospect in personal protective application. Finally, the UiO-66-NH2@ANF aerogel demonstrated high air permeability of $93.7\mathrm{mm}/s$ (standard GB/T 5453) and the moisture management tester (MMT) results also showed that the aerogel had excellent moisture transportance (Fig.S10), with the adsorption rates of
77.461 $\%\%$ and 20.755 $\%\%$ for the top layer and bottom layer, respectively (Table.S3).
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we developed a lightweight, flexible, and mechanically robust UiO-66- ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel. With high SSAs of ANFs, the UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals were anchored onto ANFs with high mass loading of $261\%$ . The UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ were assembled to a hierarchically porous architecture with good mechanical stability, with high recovery rate of $93.3\%$ after 100 cycles under $30\%$ strain. Benefit from the features, the UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel demonstrates high CEES removal rate of $98.9\%$ and short half-life of $8.15\ \mathrm{min}$ . Moreover, the UiO-66- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogel presents high thermal insulation with low $\lambda$ of $25.66\mathrm{\mW}\mathrm{m}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , high flame resistance with high LOI of $32\%$ , and good wearing comfortableness. Considering these advantages, the UiO-6 ${\mathsf{i}}6{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{A N F}}$ aerogels show promising potential in multifunctional protection against CWAs.
